  <h2>The short answer</h2>
  <p>Both calls, same day, in this order: <strong>safety, shutoff, photos, start drying, notify your insurer.</strong> The mistake that costs people money isn't calling in the wrong order. It's letting wet drywall, carpet and subfloor sit for days while waiting for an adjuster's permission, then discovering mold and a smaller payout.</p>

  <h2>Why drying can't wait for the adjuster</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mold starts fast.</strong> The EPA advises drying wet building materials and furnishings within <strong>24 to 48 hours</strong> to prevent mold growth. Adjusters, especially after storms, often can't come that fast.</li>
    <li><strong>Your policy expects it.</strong> Standard homeowners policies list "duties after loss" that include giving prompt notice and <strong>protecting the property from further damage</strong>. Damage that gets worse because nobody dried it can be harder to claim.</li>
    <li><strong>Mitigation is different from repair.</strong> Extracting water, removing soaked materials that can't be saved, and setting up drying equipment is <em>mitigation</em>. Rebuilding walls and replacing floors is <em>restoration</em>. Insurers expect the first quickly and want to agree the second.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Minute by minute</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Make it safe.</strong> Stay out of water near outlets or the electrical panel, leave if you smell gas, keep out from under sagging ceilings. Full steps in <a href="/guides/water-damage-mold-emergency/">what to do before help arrives</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Stop the water</strong> at the fixture valve or the main shutoff.</li>
    <li><strong>Document before moving anything:</strong> a walk-through video, photos of the source and waterlines, and keep the failed part.</li>
    <li><strong>Call a water mitigation company</strong> if more than a small area is wet, the water is gray or black, it reached walls, ceilings or a lower floor, or it has been wet for more than a day.</li>
    <li><strong>Notify your insurer</strong> by phone or app. Get a claim number. Ask: <em>do you need to approve the mitigation company, or will you send one?</em></li>
    <li><strong>Keep a log:</strong> who you called, when, and what they said. Save every receipt, including fans, tarps and hotel nights.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What to say when you call your insurer</h2>
  <p>A clear first call sets the tone of the claim:</p>
  <blockquote>"I'm reporting water damage at my home. It started around [time] on [date] from [source]. It affected [rooms or areas]. I've stopped the water and photographed everything. A mitigation company is [coming at time / I'd like your recommendation]. Can you give me a claim number, tell me who my adjuster will be, and tell me if you need anything before drying starts?"</blockquote>
  <p>Ask them to note in the file that you started mitigation to prevent further damage.</p>

  <h2>What to ask the restoration company</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>When can you arrive, and what will you do on the first visit?</li>
    <li>Will you take <strong>moisture readings and photos</strong> each day and share them? (Professional drying under the IICRC S500 standard is measured, not guessed.)</li>
    <li>Do you bill my insurer directly, and <strong>what happens if part of it isn't covered</strong>?</li>
    <li>What exactly am I signing? Read <a href="/guides/restoration-work-authorization-assignment-of-benefits/">work authorizations and assignments of benefits</a> before you sign anything.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When you might not file a claim at all</h2>
  <p>If the water was clean, the area is small and it can be dried within a day or two, the cost may be below your deductible, and claims can affect future premiums. Get the facts first: <a href="/guides/small-leak-dry-it-yourself-or-call-a-pro/">can you dry it yourself?</a> and <a href="/guides/water-damage-restoration-cost/">what restoration costs</a>. Telling your insurer about a loss and filing a claim for payment aren't always the same step; ask your agent how your insurer treats an inquiry.</p>

  <h2>If the insurer wants its own company</h2>
  <p>Many insurers have preferred restoration vendors. They can make approval smoother, but in most cases you can still choose your own company. The trade-offs are in <a href="/guides/insurance-preferred-vendor-or-own-contractor/">the insurer's vendor or your own contractor</a>.</p>

